export const body = "<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>This article highlights the underlying similarity between realistic and stylized art. Then, she provides the challenges of stylization and proposes two categories of stylized art. Finally, she shares her workflow for both styles, from reference analysis to sculpting and texturing.</p>\n\n<p>Original article: Realistic vs. Stylized: Technique Overview</p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span><strong>Realism and Stylization</strong></p>\n<p>Video games have been pursuing realistic graphics for a long time. When Nathan Drake debuted in 2007, his character model can be considered realistic; however, it certainly looks stylized in comparison to his 2017 iteration. The point is, what looks real today may not look real in a few years as technologies advance.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/949349ff29870e77f891.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/949349ff29870e77f891.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:547px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The games below (<em>Uncharted</em> <em>4, Horizon: Zero Dawn, The Witcher 3</em> and <em>Red Dead Redemption 2</em>) are praised for their enhanced realism. Enhanced realism means the colors, light, and atmosphere are tuned to varying degrees to produce visuals that surpass our everyday reality. In other words, even with realistic graphics, game artists are not doing a 1:1 conversion. Their goal is to craft a game world that feels believable while looking extra good.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/0eae84eff0a7ed5ed3e6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/0eae84eff0a7ed5ed3e6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>New allowances in technology such as PBR materials and higher polycounts have enabled stylized art to flourish alongside realism. While realism aims to capture reality then enhance it, stylized art creates opportunity for playful shapes and colors, as well as exaggerating or minimizing details.</p>\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><em>In conclusion, you could argue that realism is a stylization in [and] of itself and that nothing is truly realistic, but manufactured to appear realistic within today’s technological limitations.</em></p></div>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/06aba1773659a54cf782.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/06aba1773659a54cf782.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"658\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Challenges of stylization</strong></p>\n<p><strong>About simplification</strong></p>\n<p>With stylized, assets, environments and characters are typically depicted with fewer details and have a strong emphasis on shapes, colors, and forms instead. For example, one artist may stylize a birch tree in a widely different way compared to others. So, the question becomes how to simplify an object without losing its visual essence.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a78dd50350abef58e371.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a78dd50350abef58e371.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Let's say you want to simplify an electrical line. The main shape of this object comes from the wooden beams, but the insulators connecting the lines to the pole are quite important too. So, if you plan to remove the insulators, you have to ask yourself two questions:</p>\n<ul><li><em>Are the details necessary to convey the function?</em></li><li><em>Would it look silly if the details were enhanced or misinterpreted as another function?</em></li></ul>\n<p>On top of that, you also need to know <em>how close the player can view the object</em>. In this case, the insulators are installed at the top of the pole, meaning the player might not even spot them from far away. However, if the game allows them to get close, don't hesitate to add the insulators in to enhance the silhouette and vary the material palette of the object. There is no one correct answer, though it's important to <em>keep your visual language consistent throughout</em>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/78982c05965818c85e13.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/78982c05965818c85e13.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>At its core, stylization centers around simplications in shape, lines, color, pattern, surface details, functionality and relationship to other objects in a scene. In this article, the artist proposes two categories in this article: <strong>over-exaggerated stylization</strong> and <strong>minimalistic stylization</strong>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/aba41a6738cd62d2b563.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/aba41a6738cd62d2b563.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"352\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Over-exaggerated stylization</strong></p>\n<p>This category focuses on:</p>\n<ul><li>Exaggerating shapes and silhouettes</li><li>Larger shapes and no surface details</li><li>Removing small details (or enlarging them)</li></ul>\n<p>Two famous games in this category are <em>Ratchet and Clank</em> and <em>Overwatch</em>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/49f555a8bc1073c240bf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/49f555a8bc1073c240bf.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Minimalistic stylization</strong></p>\n<p>The common characteristics of this category are:</p>\n<ul><li>Larger shapes and silhouettes</li><li>Conveying a shape and an object with minimal details</li><li>Usually flat and/or limited details in normal map information</li></ul>\n<p>Two famous games in this category are <em>Firewatch</em> and <em>Journey</em>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/095531cdba363528cf50.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/095531cdba363528cf50.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Workflow and comparison </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sculpting</strong></p>\n<p>Whether it's a realistic or stylized project, the artist always starts with analyzing the reference. Sequentially, she would look into larger shapes first, then medium, small and microsurface details. This process helps her define the characteristics of the object, which in turn tells her what details to change or enhance in stylization.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/c69a712a00e845b5efd1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/c69a712a00e845b5efd1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"996\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>For this tutorial, she chose a mandarin because it has a simple silhouette. Below are the two variants for illustration: left is realistic, right stylized. They both share the same oval-round shape with an uneven surface as mandarin is a fruit. The bumpy surface results in an uneven texture which looks rough due to light scattering. Knowing the bumps are an important feature, she kept a few of them and enlarge them in her stylized version. This decision also helps distinguish the stylized mandarin from an orange tomato (which would be more glossy and smooth).</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/03d2b7884721eb939590.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/03d2b7884721eb939590.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"525\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Silhouette</strong></p>\n<p>To expand on the silhouette topic, we will now look at two variants of a wooden stump. This object's silhouette is comparatively more complex than that of the mandarin, but the principle remains unchanged: it should be recognizable at first glance. Here, the artist opted for over-exaggerated stylization.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/34097ca4df7c66e2823b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/34097ca4df7c66e2823b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"451\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Before sculpting, the artist divided the asset into 4 sections:</p>\n<ul><li>Silhouette and large shapes</li><li>Medium details</li><li>Small details</li><li>Microsurface details</li></ul>\n<p>Below, we're looking at the silhouette and large shapes. Since detailing requires a higher amount of polygons, the artist recommends starting with the overall silhouette. That way, it would be easier to iterate on the larger shapes and establish a visual framework for the rest.</p>\n<p>Comparing the two stumps below, we can see the realistic one looks more organic and wobbly in some areas, particularly at the bottom.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a6ec03e6576c987d3bab.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a6ec03e6576c987d3bab.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"452\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>As for medium details, they are smaller shapes that affect the silhouette but is not part of the base silhouette. These shapes require more polygons than the main silhouette. In the picture below, the medium shapes are the splinters on the top of the stump where the tearing takes place. So, you can ask questions like:</p>\n<ul><li>Did someone saw it off?</li><li>Did it fall, which angle did it fall from?</li></ul>\n<p>If it would have been sawed off, the fracture would look quite different. It would be flat, and the inner wood would have seen marks and little indication of tearing.</p>\n<p>The splintering and unevenness are minimized and simplified in the stylized version to keep it clean. Meanwhile the realistic one has more small “teeth” and jaggedness as to mimicking the splintered look of a real stump.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/414bd542b5389882dd42.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/414bd542b5389882dd42.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"451\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Small details don't affect the overall shape and silhouette. They only inform the surface finish of the object. For a tree stump, that information comes from the bark, which has different types. Here, the stylized stump has larger strips of the bark, as they are characteristic of a wood stump. These details were exaggerated instead of being removed.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the wood eye and the hole are not affecting the silhouette, so they are considered small details. The wood eye hints at the branch once attached to the trunk, its size indicating the proportion of the now missing component. Since the wood eye is fairly small and near the bottom of the trunk, the branch probably was young when it was removed.</p>\n<p><sub>Side note</sub><sub>: Branches tend to be larger on the bottom trunk than on the top (if not removed). These details are worth keeping in mind when creating the asset.</sub></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/b3e0c2cdbe6d4d45848e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/b3e0c2cdbe6d4d45848e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"452\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Now, we will inspect the wooden rings, which indicate the age of a tree. The stump here is quite thick, so we can assume that the tree is quite old, which means we can add more rings in there. For stylized, we can't have too many rings because it would look too noisy and grainy. Therefore, the artist left out the smaller rings to focus on the large ones.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/457ee7129dce4e146a97.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/457ee7129dce4e146a97.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"590\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The microsurface details are the last ones to be added; they are also the most expensive in terms of polygons. Coming to this stage, the asset must be free of all issues, major or minor, so that no significant tweaks would be required.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/9a9d43e612efdac53094.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/9a9d43e612efdac53094.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"449\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>In this example, the realistic bark has a fibrous structure, with flakes layering on top of each other. To de-noise the stylized surface, the artist focused on large shapes and got rid of surface noise such as flaky pieces.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/d5bd0ecdbc298c2b4bad.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/d5bd0ecdbc298c2b4bad.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"561\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Below is the final UV comparison between the realistic stump and the stylized stump.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/1210a69f6f4b2f4923da.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/1210a69f6f4b2f4923da.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"575\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Texturing</strong></p>\n<p>At first glance, stylized textures might seem easier to make compared to realistic textures. But that is not necessarily the case. Before, artists had to handpaint light and shadow information in their textures, but nowadays with PBR being prevalent in both realistic and stylized projects, the process has become standardized.</p>\n<p>In PBR, you have a flat color input for albedo with no light nor shadow information, which means manual painting of highlights and shadows is no longer necessary. As a result, using PBR essentially homogenized the texturing process across both styles, making neither of them harder nor easier.</p>\n<p>The meshes below show the final textures with light information. They only have the base input: Albedo, Normal, Gloss, Specular and Ambient Occlusion. This is to demonstrate the basic principle of modeling and texturing without advanced inputs.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/c7712d95404abca4a458.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/c7712d95404abca4a458.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"462\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The picture below showcases the same wood stump, but with only Albedo input and an unlit material. Most of the medium, small and microsurface details are only visible through a viewport with scene lighting thanks to the light information and depth from the normal, gloss and specular inputs combined with the light source (which are lost when removing these inputs).</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/8829e78d6a08f7daa7c5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/8829e78d6a08f7daa7c5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"462\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Below are albedo maps for both the stylized and realistic wood stump to offer a better overview when comparing the texturing methods for both assets. In PBR, you leave the albedo flat with only color information as highlights and shadows are calculated by the rendering engine. You can see that the artist added highlights and shadows to the albedo of the stylized wood stump to emphasize depth.</p>\n<p>Specifically, she used a curvature map of roughly 5% strength, and overlayed in a brown color to add some dirt to the crevices. This is largely her style choice, and she said it is fine to experiment with details as long as you don't break the lighting in the scene. Since different engines have different methods of PBR with varied inputs, it mostly serves as guidelines—not rules.</p>\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><em>It’s easy to forget that these are not set rules, and as an artist, you have the artistic freedom to enhance and emphasize elements in your work.</em></p></div>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/e30f8e03c78c91cc659f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/e30f8e03c78c91cc659f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"577\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The picture below displays the same two stumps in different lighting setups. Have a look and compare them to see how the stylized asset differs from the realistic asset.</p>\n<p>The added highlights and shadows in the stylized stump didn't ruin the cast shadows coming from different directions. Likewise, the realistic stump didn't alter the shadow casting although it has added brown color in the crevices. The addition, in fact, enhanced the details of the bark in dull lighting conditions.</p>\n<p>However, if too much highlight or shadow were added to the albedo, it might've broken the shadow casting, which in turn makes the asset not reusable in various lighting conditions.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/bee5c720864d46bc8ef7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/bee5c720864d46bc8ef7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"294\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>Advice</strong></p>\n<p>First things first, you should always analyze your references before starting a project. Break it down thoroughly before you go into sculpting.</p>\n<p>Breaking it down means dividing it into large, medium, small and microsurface details. This step ensures the final product will retain the essence of its origin. By establishing a strong clear hierarchy of details early on, you can reduce potential errors in the latter steps.</p>\n<p>Of course, if you are working on a stylized project, you should define whether it is over-exaggerated or minimalistic stylization. This stylistic direction guides the way you handle silhouette and detailing.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><em>\"As a final conclusion, remember that there are no correct ways of creating assets for neither realism or stylized, what matters is the end results; how successfully you managed to communicate the core and essence of the assets in the game to the players.\"</em></p></div>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span><em>Thank you for reading and happy creating!</em></p></div>";
export const images = ["assets/cookies-inline/03d2b7884721eb939590.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/06aba1773659a54cf782.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/095531cdba363528cf50.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/0eae84eff0a7ed5ed3e6.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/1210a69f6f4b2f4923da.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/34097ca4df7c66e2823b.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/414bd542b5389882dd42.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/457ee7129dce4e146a97.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/49f555a8bc1073c240bf.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/78982c05965818c85e13.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/8829e78d6a08f7daa7c5.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/949349ff29870e77f891.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/9a9d43e612efdac53094.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a6ec03e6576c987d3bab.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a78dd50350abef58e371.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/aba41a6738cd62d2b563.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/b3e0c2cdbe6d4d45848e.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/bee5c720864d46bc8ef7.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/c69a712a00e845b5efd1.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/c7712d95404abca4a458.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/d5bd0ecdbc298c2b4bad.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/e30f8e03c78c91cc659f.webp"];
